export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="cli-server">
  <p>
    <code>vector serve</code> runs the agent as a headless server with <code>--port</code> and <code>--hostname</code>;
    <code>vector web</code> starts the same server and opens the browser interface. <code>vector attach &lt;url&gt;</code>
    connects a terminal session to a running server, with <code>--dir</code> to choose its directory and
    <code>--username</code> and <code>--password</code> for Basic authentication.
  </p>
  <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector serve --port 4096
vector attach http://localhost:4096 --dir ~/code/myapp</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>
  <p>
    Configure <code>VECTOR_SERVER_PASSWORD</code> and, optionally, <code>VECTOR_SERVER_USERNAME</code>;
    the default username is <code>vector</code>. Non-loopback binding requires a password unless you explicitly
    choose <code>--unsecured</code>. Use a protected secret source for server passwords. Settings and database
    overrides are <code>VECTOR_AGENT_CONFIG</code>, <code>VECTOR_AGENT_CONFIG_DIR</code> and <code>VECTOR_AGENT_DB</code>.
    Keep the existing database file when migrating an ambiguous historical override.
  </p>
  <h3>Upcoming native installation</h3>
  <p>
    The unversioned candidate prepares Vector shell and PowerShell installers that verify native archives and
    do not require Node.js or npm. <code>vector upgrade</code> and <code>vector uninstall</code> respect the verified
    installer or package manager that owns the running executable. On Windows, replacement of a running executable
    is scheduled after it exits; scheduled does not mean completed. Installer publication and Windows acceptance
    are still pending. Use the <a href="/releases">published release instructions</a> for available downloads.
  </p>
  <h3>Windows Subsystem for Linux</h3>
  <p>
    In the candidate, Install Vector in server settings uses the native Vector installer with a separately pinned,
    exact CLI version required by the desktop. It installs <code>~/.vector/bin/vector-native</code> using Linux
    download, archive and SHA-256 tools. Node.js and npm are not required. A matching installed engine can start
    without the download tools; a version mismatch requires an update.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sign in to the desktop before starting its managed WSL server. The desktop passes its Vector account token
    through the managed process's stdin and stops stale processes when the account changes or signs out. It does
    not put the token in command arguments, an installer receipt or a new credential file. Provider credentials
    remain separate; connect your chosen provider inside that environment when needed. Startup binds to Linux
    loopback, checks health and requires an unauthenticated request to be rejected before connecting. Actual
    Windows/WSL and published-installer validation remain release requirements.
  </p>
  <h3>Public transcripts and teams</h3>
  <p>
    The candidate restores public transcript sharing on Vector's service. Preview the content, explicitly choose
    publication and future updates, and set an expiry before sharing. Public links can expose visible prompts,
    code and tool output; importing creates passive local history. Removing a new Vector share does not remove a
    historical copy on another service. <code>vector invite</code> remains the separate live-workspace feature.
  </p>
  <p>
    Vector Teams offers <code>vector org list</code>, <code>vector org switch &lt;orgID&gt;</code> and
    <code>vector org personal</code>. A selected team supplies signed provider, permission and integration defaults;
    Personal clears selection even while offline. Refresh running apps after changing the selection from a separate
    CLI. Sharing and Teams require their production service setup before availability can be announced.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>vector acp</code> starts an Agent Client Protocol server for compatible editors. For programmatic HTTP
    use, see the <a href="/docs/sdk">SDK guide</a>; first public SDK publication remains pending.
  </p>
</DocsPage>
